# Local development, tests, and deployment helpers are intentionally
# excluded from the published Hermes plugin checkout. Hermes installs this
# repository directly into $HERMES_HOME/plugins/clawchat, so keep GitHub's
# tracked tree focused on runtime plugin files plus repository documentation.
.e2e/
# Ignored wholesale EXCEPT the named checks below, which pin invariants no
# reviewer or CI can otherwise see. `conftest.py` is required by all of them:
# it installs the minimal Hermes host double, so the tracked tests run on a
# plain `pip install -e .[test]` checkout with no Hermes anywhere.
#   * multiplex_isolation — per-profile store/supervisor/sender/credential
#     isolation, invisible to any single-profile run. Needs a REAL Hermes on
#     PYTHONPATH and skips itself without one.
#   * device_id_scope / activate_device_id / activate_precheck — device-id
#     derivation and its paired-device compatibility rules, which a caching
#     change can silently break (it did: see 642be46).
#   * pre_dispatch_self_echo — the self-echo hook resolves the bot id of the
#     event's OWN profile under multiplexing, not the launch profile's.
#   * restart_outcome — the post-activation restart pre-check, its cwd, and the
#     captured outcome log; a regression here is silent by construction.
#   * command_gating — non-owners cannot run gateway/admin slash commands in a
#     direct chat, cannot control the gateway with plain text, may answer their
#     own /new confirm but never with "always", and group commands are judged
#     before the mention-only gate. A security invariant: a regression reopens
#     remote /restart or a config rewrite for anyone.
#   * register_platform_cron — cron_deliver_env_var reaches the host, with the
#     graded TypeError fallback for older Hermes; failures only show up as
#     cron jobs silently ending `blocked_config`.
#   * install_scan — the tracked tree passes Hermes' own install-time scanner
#     (`hermes plugins install`, plugins.scan_on_install). One CRITICAL finding
#     anywhere, tests included, is a hard block --force cannot override. Needs a
#     Hermes source checkout and skips itself without one.
#   * memory_defaults — activation writes, and plugin load fills in, the host
#     memory keys that stop Hermes' single-user memory (USER.md, background
#     review) from collecting every ClawChat sender; load never overwrites an
#     operator's value.
#   * memory_append_dedupe — appending a note paragraph the file already
#     holds does not add it a second time, and the tool result says so.
#   * note_injection — a turn shows the agent's own notes for the people and
#     group in it, within note-cap-user / note-cap-group / note-cap-turn, and
#     never shows owner.md outside the owner's direct chat.
#   * memory_read_scope — the memory tools return owner.md only in the
#     owner's direct chat, and in a group only that group's note and its
#     members' notes: a tool result stays in the group's shared session for
#     anyone in it. An unplaceable session reads nothing. A privacy invariant.
#   * memory_routing_text — the platform hint, the clawchat_memory_write
#     description and a one-line reminder in every turn's channel prompt (which
#     also reaches sessions started before the hint had the rule) say facts
#     about a person / group / the owner go to their ClawChat note, not to
#     Hermes' MEMORY.md.
#   * group_shared_session — a group is one Hermes session for every speaker
#     by default, even though Hermes fills group_sessions_per_user=true into
#     every platform's runtime extra; only a per-speaker setting written in
#     config.yaml is honoured, with a one-time warning; direct chats keep one
#     session per conversation.
#   * group_session_seed / group_turn_queue — a group's new shared session is
#     seeded once with recent group messages, later mentions only add what the
#     session has not seen, and a batch that arrives while the group's turn is
#     running waits and goes out as the next batch instead of reaching
#     Hermes' version-dependent busy handling.
#   * session_sediment — before /new and before compression a silent turn
#     saves the conversation's facts into only that conversation's ClawChat
#     notes; its output never reaches the chat, and /new is never delayed for
#     a busy session. In a shared group it also carries the group messages the
#     session has not seen, so a "don't remember that" is in front of it.
#   * typing_cadence — every host typing refresh (2 s) reaches the wire, so
#     the receiver's indicator window never lapses mid-reply; false follows the
#     turn and graceful shutdown; a sediment turn sends no typing.
#   * message_length — a long reply is one message (32000 chars), and a
#     full-length one still fits the hub's per-message byte cap.
#   * thinking_mode — tool progress, reasoning, status and notices go out as
#     message_mode "thinking", the reply as "normal"; another agent's
#     non-normal message is never conversational input; tool previews and
#     terminal commands are rendered as code.
#   * text_mentions — "@name" typed in a group reply becomes a mention
#     fragment plus context.mentions, with the reference client's rules (e-mail
#     guard, longest match, ties link nobody, never self or "all").
#   * activation_websocket_url — activation records the installer's
#     CLAWCHAT_WEBSOCKET_URL as extra.websocket_url and derives one from the
#     base URL only when none is set.
#   * silence_markers — a bare NO_REPLY / [SILENT] / HEARTBEAT_OK reply is
#     suppressed, but only as the whole reply; prose that mentions one is
#     sent. Mirrors the OpenClaw plugin's rule-B cases.
#   * reply_streaming — opt-in (extra.stream_replies, default off): a
#     direct-chat reply streams (created/add/done) and ends as one
#     message.reply under the same id; groups are not streamed; held, failed
#     and abandoned streams. Off: never a stream frame, even with host
#     streaming on; activation and presets keep host streaming off.
#   * agent_files / liveware_account — every Hermes profile on a host is its
#     own agent: greeting.md / friend-greeting.md / onboarding.json are read
#     from $HERMES_HOME/clawchat/ (only the default profile falls back to the
#     shared ~/clawchat/), and every liveware CLI process names the agent's own
#     --account in the host-wide CLI login store.
#   * moment_images — clawchat_create_moment passes http(s) image URLs,
#     uploads an existing absolute local image file and publishes its URL, and
#     rejects anything else without creating a moment.
#   * cross_chat_file_send — a file sent with send_message (MEDIA:) into a chat
#     the turn just mentioned is delivered, not swallowed by the mention's
#     terminal marker, which still drops the real follow-up reply;
#     [[as_document]] survives as a file fragment.
#   * friend_greeting_nickname — the greeting to a new friend refreshes their
#     profile first (bounded) and carries their nickname as sender_name.
#   * session_names — a direct chat's Hermes session is named after the peer
#     (chat_name and user_name), a group's after its title with no user_name
#     on the shared session.
#   * permission_result_receipt — a permission receipt's result object reaches
#     the agent; approved = done, do not call again; only approved_retry asks
#     for one more call; refusals never retry; unknown outcomes are not
#     refusals; window-settled receipts make no turn.
#   * inbound_rate_warning — the rate-spike warning counts only messages that
#     passed the self-echo drop and the message-id dedupe.
#   * tool_media_host — the tools' REST uploads (/media/upload) go to the
#     media host (media base URL, else the WebSocket host), not the API host;
#     avatar uploads stay on the API host.
#   * inbound_media_download — inbound attachment downloads send a
#     non-default User-Agent (the CDN 403s urllib's), and a failed download
#     is logged without the URL's query string.
#   * send_file_tool — clawchat_send_file sends a local file into any chat by
#     id (as_document, caption), is not swallowed after a same-turn mention,
#     refuses credential paths, and falls back to the standalone sender.
#   * inbound_dispatch_lanes — inbound messages are handled off the WS read
#     loop, per chat and in order. A handler that awaits a send's ack inside
#     the read loop can never get it: the read loop stalls for the ack timeout,
#     the connection is rebuilt, and frames for every chat are lost meanwhile.
#   * receipt_replay_dedup — a permission receipt or moment-comment note
#     replayed after a gateway restart starts no second turn (ledger claim).
